ataset.\n5. The mean of the new dataset is greater than the mean of the original dataset, and the median of the new dataset is less than the median of the original dataset."] [:db/cas 17592186208478 :question/worked-solution "When a new data point is added, the mean of the dataset will change, and the median of the dataset may change:\n\n- The mean of the dataset moves toward that new data point.\n- The median of the dataset moves toward that new data point or stays the same.\n\nSince the original mean lies around $7$, and the new data point $20$ is much larger, the mean of the new dataset must be *greater* than the original mean.\n\nNow, to compare the medians, we first calculate the cumulative frequencies.\n\n| Bitrate | Number of Sessions | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $4$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $5$ | $7$ | $13$ |\n| $6$ | $8$ | $21$ |\n| $7$ | $8$ | $29$ |\n| $8$ | $10$ | $39$ |\n| $9$ | $9$ | $48$ |\n| $10$ | $9$ | $57$ |\n\nIn the original dataset of $57$ sessions, the middle is the $29$th value. From the cumulative frequencies, the $29$th value corresponds to a bitrate of $7$. Thus, the median is $7$.\n\nWhen the $20$ is added, the dataset size becomes $58$, so the middle lies between the $29$th and $30$th values. These values correspond to bitrates of $7$ and $8$, respectively. Thus, the median is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7+8}{2}=7.5\n$$\n\nwhich is greater than $7$.\n\nTherefore, the correct comparison of the two datasets is option IV, which states that the mean of the new dataset is greater than the mean of the original dataset, and the median of the new dataset is greater than the median of the original dataset." "When a new data point is added, the mean of the dataset will change, and the median of the dataset may change:\n\n- The mean of the dataset moves toward that new data point.\n- The median of the dataset moves toward that new data point or stays the same.\n\nSince the original mean lies around $7$, and the new data point $20$ is much larger, the mean of the new dataset must be *greater* than the original mean.\n\nNow, to compare the medians, we first calculate the cumulative frequencies.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Bitrate</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Sessions</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$29$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$39$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$48$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$57$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIn the original dataset of $57$ sessions, the middle is the $29$th value. From the cumulative frequencies, the $29$th value corresponds to a bitrate of $7$. Thus, the median is $7$.\n\nWhen the $20$ is added, the dataset size becomes $58$, so the middle lies between the $29$th and $30$th values. These values correspond to bitrates of $7$ and $8$, respectively. Thus, the median is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7+8}{2}=7.5\n$$\n\nwhich is greater than $7$.\n\nTherefore, the correct comparison of the two datasets is option IV, which states that the mean of the new dataset is greater than the mean of the original dataset, and the median of the new dataset is greater than the median of the original dataset."] [:db/cas 17592186208480 :question/problem "The table shows heart rates (beats per minute) measured at six time intervals during a workout.\n\n| Interval | Heart Rate (bpm) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 1 | $120$ |\n| 2 | $125$ |\n| 3 | $118$ |\n| 4 | $122$ |\n| 5 | $127$ |\n| 6 | $121$ |\n\nA seventh interval measured $140$ bpm, and this value is added to the original dataset of six time intervals to create a new dataset. Which of the following statements correctly compares the means of the two datasets?\n\n1. The mean of the original dataset is greater than the mean of the new dataset.\n2. The mean of the original dataset is less than the mean of the new dataset.\n3. The means of both datasets are equal.\n4. There is not enough information to compare the means.\n5. Both means are undefined." "The table shows heart rates (beats per minute) measured at six time intervals during a workout.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Interval</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Heart Rate (bpm)</th></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$120$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$125$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$118$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$122$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$127$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$121$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nA seventh interval measured $140$ bpm, and this value is added to the original dataset of six time intervals to create a new dataset. Which of the following statements correctly compares the means of the two datasets?\n\n1. The mean of the original dataset is greater than the mean of the new dataset.\n2. The mean of the original dataset is less than the mean of the new dataset.\n3. The means of both datasets are equal.\n4. There is not enough information to compare the means.\n5. Both means are undefined."] [:db/cas 17592186208487 :question/problem "The table below shows the distribution, by location and power capacity (maximum rate of power generation), of the twenty largest geothermal plants in the United States in $2022$.\n\n| State | Power capacity |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Low | Medium | High | Total |  |\n| Nevada | $2$ | $3$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| California | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| Utah | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| Idaho | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| Oregon | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| New Mexico | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| Arizona | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| Total | $6$ | $9$ | $5$ | $20$ |\n\nThe total power capacity of the three plants located in Utah was $1350$ megawatts, and the total power capacity of the twenty plants was $8450$ megawatts in $2022$. The amount of energy produced in one hour at a rate of one megawatt is one megawatt-hour.\n\nIf each of the three Utah plants in $2022$ had operated continuously for $24$ hours at the maximum rate of power generation, approximately how many megawatt-hours of energy would the three plants have produced?\n\n1. $35,000$\n2. $32,000$\n3. $30,000$" "The table below shows the distribution, by location and power capacity (maximum rate of power generation), of the twenty large